{
  "id": "rad-03-01",
  "slug": "roots-as-exponents",
  "title": "Roots as Exponents",
  "courseId": "radicals-and-exponents",
  "chapterId": "ch3-rational-exponents",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "c1",
      "kind": "concept",
      "figure": "radical-factor",
      "body": "A fractional exponent is a root: a^(1/n) means the n-th root of a. So a^(1/2) = √a and a^(1/3) = ∛a. For example, 16^(1/2) = 4 because 4² = 16, and 27^(1/3) = 3 because 3³ = 27."
    },
    {
      "id": "i1",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "A one-half power.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "expLogExplore",
        "mode": "exponential",
        "x": 2,
        "targetBase": 4,
        "startBase": 2,
        "showMirror": false,
        "prompt": "16^(1/2) asks for a square root. Reframe it forward: find the base whose SQUARE is 16.",
        "successFeedback": "Base 4, so 4² = 16 — and that is exactly what 16^(1/2) = 4 means, read the other way. Raising to the 1/2 power and squaring undo each other, so the two questions are the same question asked from opposite ends.",
        "lowFeedback": "base² is still under 16.",
        "highFeedback": "base² has passed 16."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "k1",
      "kind": "check",
      "body": "A cube root.",
      "conceptTag": "rad-unit-fraction-exp",
      "explanationVariants": [
        "27^(1/3) = ∛27 = 3, since 3³ = 27.",
        "The 1/3 power is the cube root: 3³ = 27, so the answer is 3."
      ],
      "widget": {
        "type": "exactNumberLab",
        "prompt": "What is 27^(1/3)?",
        "task": "rationalExponentEvaluate",
        "values": [
          {
            "id": "power",
            "label": "27^(1/3)",
            "kind": "power",
            "base": 27,
            "exponent": 1,
            "rootIndex": 3
          }
        ],
        "answerMode": "numeric",
        "tolerance": 0,
        "numericErrors": [
          {
            "value": 9,
            "feedback": "9 is 27 ÷ 3, not the cube root. ∛27 = 3, since 3³ = 27."
          },
          {
            "value": 81,
            "feedback": "That's 27 × 3, the wrong direction. The 1/3 power is the cube root: 3."
          }
        ],
        "choices": [],
        "authoredStages": [],
        "requiredStageKeys": [],
        "requiredExplorations": 1,
        "explorationFeedback": "Inspect the required exact-number states before checking.",
        "fallbackFeedback": "27^(1/3) = ∛27 = 3.",
        "successFeedback": "27^(1/3) = ∛27 = 3."
      },
      "variant": {
        "gen": "a1-radicals",
        "form": "rad-unit-fraction-exp__numeric"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "The **denominator picks the root**: 1/4 means the fourth root, 1/5 the fifth root. So 81^(1/4) = 3 because 3⁴ = 81. Ask yourself: what number, raised to the n-th power, gives a?",
      "figure": "rad-denom-root"
    },
    {
      "id": "i2",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "A fourth root.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "What is 81^(1/4)?",
        "answer": 3,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 20,
            "feedback": "81^(1/4) isn't 81 ÷ 4. It's the fourth root: 3⁴ = 81, so the answer is 3."
          },
          {
            "value": 9,
            "feedback": "9 is the square root (81^(1/2)). The fourth root is 3, since 3⁴ = 81."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "81^(1/4) = 3, since 3⁴ = 81."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "This works for any perfect power. 32^(1/5) = 2 because 2⁵ = 32. A rational exponent is just a root written as a fraction — the two notations mean exactly the same thing.",
      "figure": "rad-perfect-power"
    },
    {
      "id": "i3",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "A fifth root.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "What is 32^(1/5)?",
        "answer": 2,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 6,
            "feedback": "32^(1/5) isn't about dividing. It's the fifth root: 2⁵ = 32, so the answer is 2."
          },
          {
            "value": 16,
            "feedback": "16 is 32 ÷ 2. The fifth root is 2, since 2⁵ = 32."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "32^(1/5) = 2, since 2⁵ = 32."
      }
    },
